00:00:54,460 --> 00:01:01,380
A discussion for which I shall remain grateful to him as long as I live.

12
00:01:01,380 --> 00:01:06,079
The point was that I was supposed to study theoretical physics at University

13
00:01:06,079 --> 00:01:11,099
of Lead, and simultaneously, and as I found two activities harder and

14
00:01:11,099 --> 00:01:15,978
harder to combine, I had to make up my mind either to stop programming and

15
00:01:15,978 --> 00:01:21,839
become a real respectable, theoretical physicist, or to carry my study of

16
00:01:21,839 --> 00:01:26,616
physics to a formal completion only with minimum of efforts and to become, yes.

17
00:01:26,616 --> 00:01:37,400
A programmer, but was that a respectable profession for

18
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:38,739
after all it was programming?

19
00:01:38,739 --> 00:01:42,039
Where was the sound body of knowledge that could support it as an

20
00:01:42,039 --> 00:01:44,439
intellectually respectable discipline?

21
00:01:44,439 --> 00:01:47,257
I remember quite vividly how I envied my hardware colleagues.

22
00:01:47,257 --> 00:01:55,758
Who when asked about their professional competence could at least point out

23
00:01:55,758 --> 00:02:01,079
that they knew everything about vacuum tubes and amplifiers and the rest.

24
00:02:01,079 --> 00:02:04,680
Whereas I felt that when faced with that question, I would

25
00:02:04,680 --> 00:02:05,454
stand handed full of misgivings.

26
00:02:05,454 --> 00:02:11,137
I knocked on my friend's office door asking him whether I could

27
00:02:11,137 --> 00:02:17,279
speak to him for a moment when I left his office a number of hours.

28
00:02:17,279 --> 00:02:22,318
I was another person for after having listened to my problems patiently, he

29
00:02:22,318 --> 00:02:28,459
agreed that up till that moment, there wasn't much programming discipline.

30
00:02:28,459 --> 00:02:32,920
But then he went on to explain quietly that automatic computers were here to

31
00:02:32,920 --> 00:02:36,355
