such profession  and believe it or.

43
00:03:28,939 --> 00:03:32,897
But under the Heading Profession, my Marriage Act shows the ridiculous

44
00:03:32,897 --> 00:03:38,935
entry, theoretical physicist, so much for the slowness with which

45
00:03:38,935 --> 00:03:45,800
I was, I saw the programming profession emerge in my own country.

46
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:49,098
Since then, I have seen more of the world, and it's my general impression

47
00:03:49,098 --> 00:03:54,938
that in other countries, apart from a possible shift of dates, the growth

48
00:03:54,938 --> 00:03:55,313
pattern has been very much the.

49
00:03:55,313 --> 00:04:04,080
Let me try to capture the situation in those old days in a little bit

50
00:04:04,080 --> 00:04:06,175
more detail in the hope of getting a better understanding of the situation.

51
00:04:06,175 --> 00:04:13,297
Today, while we pursue our analysis, we shall see how many common

52
00:04:13,297 --> 00:04:17,174
misunderstandings about the true nature of the programming task can be

53
00:04:17,174 --> 00:04:23,639
traced back to that now distant past.

54
00:04:23,639 --> 00:04:27,497
The first automatic electronic computers were all unique single copy

55
00:04:27,497 --> 00:04:34,139
machines, and they were all to be found in an environment with the exciting

56
00:04:34,139 --> 00:04:36,840
flavor of an experimental laboratory.

57
00:04:36,840 --> 00:04:40,037
Once the vision of the automatic computer was there, its realization

58
00:04:40,037 --> 00:04:45,157
was a tremendous challenge to the electronic technology then available.

59
00:04:45,157 --> 00:04:52,800
And one thing is, We cannot deny the courage of the groups that decided

60
00:04:52,800 --> 00:04:59,099
to try and build such a fantastic piece of equipment for a fantastic

61
00:04:59,099 --> 00:05:01,139
pieces of equipment they were.

62
00:05:01,139 --> 00:05:06,400
In retrospect, one can only wonder that those first machines

63
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:07,013
worked at all, at least sometimes.

64
00:05:07,013 --> 00:05:13,177
The overwhelming problem was to get and keep the machine in working order.

65
00:05:13,177 --> 00:05:20,219
The preoccupation with the physical aspects of automatic computing is

66
00:05:20,219 --> 00:05:22,034
still reflected in the names of the older scientific societies.

67
00:05:25,054 --> 00:05:26,456
What about a poor programmer?

68
00:05:27,750 --> 00:05:30,120
Well, to tell the honest truth.

69
00:05:30,120 --> 00:05:32,196
He was hardly noticed.

70
00:05:33,733 --> 00:05:41,120
For one thing, the first machines were so bulky that you could hardly move them,

71
00:05:41,120 --> 00:05:45,400
and besides that, they required such extensive maintenance that it was quite

72
00:05:45,400 --> 00:05:48,476
natural that the place where people tried to use the machine was the same laboratory

73
00:05:48,476 --> 00:05:51,497
where the machine had been developed.

74
00:05:51,497 --> 00:05:55,838
Secondly, his somewhat invisible work was without any glamor.

75
00:05:55,838 --> 00:06:01,658
You could show the machine to visitors, and that was several orders of

76
00:06:01,658 --> 00:06:07,916
magnitude more spectacular than some sheets of coding, but most important

77
00:06:07,916 --> 00:06:13,435
of all, the programmer himself had a very modest view of his own work.

78
00:06:13,435 --> 00:06:21,979
His work deriv all its significance from the existence of that wonderful machine,

79
00:06:21,979 --> 00:06:24,558
because that was a unique machine.

80
00:06:24,558 --> 00:06:32,680
He knew only too well that his programs had only local significance, and also

81
00:06:32,680 --> 00:06:35,057
because it was patently obvious that this machine would have a limited lifetime.

82
00:06:35,057 --> 00:06:39,157
He knew that very little of his work would have a lasting.

83
00:06:41,553 --> 00:06:46,939
Finally, there's yet another circumstance that had a profound influence on the

84
00:06:46,939 --> 00:06:47,200
programmer's attitude towards his work.

85
00:06:47,200 --> 00:06:56,560
On the one hand, besides being unreliable, his machine was usually too slow and

86
00:06:56,560 --> 00:06:56,760
his memory was usually too small.

87
00:06:56,760 --> 00:07:04,640
That is, he was faced with a pinching shoe while on the other hand, it's usually

88
00:07:04,640 --> 00:07:08,376
somewhat queer order code would cater for the most unexpected construction.

89
00:07:08,376 --> 00:07:14,338
And in those days, many a clever programmer derived an immense

90
00:07:14,338 --> 00:07:21,319
intellectual satisfaction from the cunning tricks by means of which he

91
00:07:21,319 --> 00:07:25,138
contrived to squeeze the impossible into the constraints of his equipment.

92
00:07:25,138 --> 00:07:28,577
Two opinions about programming date from those days.

93
00:07:28,577 --> 00:07:35,160
I mention them now I shall return to them.

94
00:07:35,160 --> 00:07:42,000
The one opinion was that a really competent programmer should be puzzle

95
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:44,699
minded and very fond of clever tricks.

96
00:07:44,699 --> 00:07:48,320
The other opinion was that programming was nothing more than optimizing

97
00:07:48,320 --> 00:07:55,839
the efficiency of the computational process in one direction or the other.

98
00:07:55,839 --> 00:08:00,739
The letter of opinion was the result of the frequent circumstance that

99
00:08:00,739 --> 00:08:06,620
indeed the available equipment was often a painfully pinching shoe.

100
00:08:06,620 --> 00:08:10,679
And in those days, one often encountered the naive expectation

101
00:08:10,679 --> 00:08:16,540
that once more powerful machines were available, programming would

102
00:08:16,540 --> 00:08:18,459
no longer be a problem for them.

103
00:08:18,459 --> 00:08:22,920
The struggle to push the machine to its limits would no longer be necessary, and

104
00:08:22,920 --> 00:08:23,120
that was all what programming was about.

105
00:08:23,120 --> 00:08:31,119
But in the next decade, something completely different happened.

106
00:08:31,119 --> 00:08:35,698
More powerful machines became available, not just an order of

107
00:08:35,698 --> 00:08:42,560
magnitude, more powerful, even several orders of magnitude more powerful.

108
00:08:42,560 --> 00:08:46,117
But instead of finding ourselves in the state of eternal bliss of all programming

109
00:08:46,117 --> 00:08:53,498
problems solved, we found ourselves up to our necks in the software crisis.

110
00:08:53,498 --> 00:08:53,698
How come

111
00:08:56,170 --> 00:09:00,480
there is a minor cause in one or two respects?

112
00:09:00,480 --> 00:09:05,239
Model machinery is basically more difficult to handle

113
00:09:05,239 --> 00:09:05,439
than the old machinery.

114
00:09:06,293 --> 00:09:11,919
Firstly, we've got the IO interrupts occurring at

115
00:09:11,919 --> 00:09:12,119
unpredictable and irec moments

116
00:09:14,313 --> 00:09:19,679
compared with the old sequential machine that pretended to be

117
00:09:19,679 --> 00:09:19,879
a fully deterministic ato.

118
00:09:19,879 --> 00:09:28,599
This has been a dramatic change and many systems programmers, gray hair

119
00:09:28,599 --> 00:09:33,138
bears witness to the facts that we shouldn't talk lightly about the logical

120
00:09:33,138 --> 00:09:37,260
problems created by that feature.

121
00:09:37,260 --> 00:09:43,539
Secondly, we've got machines equipped with multi-level stores presenting US

122
00:09:43,539 --> 00:09:49,420
problems of management strategy that in spite of the extensive literature on the

123
00:09:49,420 --> 00:09:56,259
subject, still remain rather elusive so much for the added complication due to

124
00:09:56,259 --> 00:09:59,160
structural changes of the actual machines.

125
00:09:59,160 --> 00:10:06,178
But I called this a minor cause the major cause is that the machines have become

126
00:10:06,178 --> 00:10:08,480
several orders of magnitude more powerful.

127
00:10:08,480 --> 00:10:13,598
To put it quite bluntly, as long as there were no machines,

128
00:10:13,598 --> 00:10:14,616
programming was no problem at all.

129
00:10:22,933 --> 00:10:29,200
And when we had a few weak computers, programming became a mild problem,

130
00:10:29,200 --> 00:10:29,417
and now we have gigantic computers.

131
00:10:29,417 --> 00:10:31,513
Programming has become an equally gigantic problem.

132
00:10:31,513 --> 00:10:36,377
In this sense, the electronic industry hasn't solved a single problem.

133
00:10:36,377 --> 00:10:39,398
It has only created them.

134
00:10:39,398 --> 00:10:46,020
It has created the problem of using its products to put it in another way,

135
00:10:46,020 --> 00:10:50,359
as the power of available machines grew by a factor of thousand or.

136
00:10:50,359 --> 00:10:57,200
Society's ambition to apply these machines grew in proportion, and it

137
00:10:57,200 --> 00:11:01,058
was the poor programmer who found his job in this exploded field of tension

138
00:11:01,058 --> 00:11:08,179
between ends and means the increased power of the hardware together with

139
00:11:08,179 --> 00:11:11,536
the perhaps even more dramatic increase in it's reliability made solutions

140
00:11:11,536 --> 00:11:19,099
feasible that the program, I hadn't dare to dream about a few years before.

141
00:11:19,099 --> 00:11:25,359
And now a few years later, he had to dream about them, and even worse, he had

142
00:11:25,359 --> 00:11:29,160
to transform such dreams into reality.

143
00:11:29,160 --> 00:11:33,460
Is it a wonder that we found ourselves in a software crisis?

144
00:11:33,460 --> 00:11:36,200
No, certainly not.

145
00:11:36,200 --> 00:11:39,538
And as you may guess, it was even predicted well in advance.

146
00:11:39,538 --> 00:11:43,397
But the trouble with minor profits, of course, is that it's only five years later

147
00:11:43,397 --> 00:11:45,232
that you really know that they had been.

148
00:11:48,232 --> 00:11:53,637
Then in the mid sixties, something terrible happened.

149
00:11:55,833 --> 00:11:59,797
The computers of the so-called third generation made their appearance.

150
00:11:59,797 --> 00:12:08,259
The official literature tells us that their price performance ratio has been

151
00:12:08,259 --> 00:12:11,219
one of the major design objectives.

152
00:12:11,219 --> 00:12:15,738
But if you take as performance, the duty cycle of the machine's, various

153
00:12:15,738 --> 00:12:22,760
components, little will prevent you from ending up with design and which

154
00:12:22,760 --> 00:12:24,494
the major part of your performance goal is reached by internal housekeeping

155
00:12:24,494 --> 00:12:26,813
activities of doubtful necessity.

156
00:12:26,813 --> 00:12:33,918
And if your definition of price is the price to be paid for the hardware, little

157
00:12:33,918 --> 00:12:37,835
will prevent you from ending up with a design that it's terribly hard to program.

158
00:12:37,835 --> 00:12:45,899
For instance, the auto code might be such as to enforce either upon the

159
00:12:45,899 --> 00:12:51,578
programmer or upon the system, early binding decisions, presenting conflicts

160
00:12:51,578 --> 00:12:54,459
that really cannot be resolved.

161
00:12:54,459 --> 00:12:58,137
And to a large extent, these unpleasant possibilities seem to have become reality

162
00:13:00,152 --> 00:13:08,160
when these machines were announced and their functional specifications became known.

163
00:13:08,160 --> 00:13:11,878
Quite a few among us must have become quite miserable.

164
00:13:11,878 --> 00:13:14,979
At least I was.

165
00:13:14,979 --> 00:13:20,300
It was only reasonable to expect that such machines would flood the

166
00:13:20,300 --> 00:13:24,779
